  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14099811",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-80642",
      "knowledge_point_id": "307bccb6-036f-51bb-99ce-327aad8dfdbe",
      "knowledge_point": "Evaluating a Double Integral When the Integrand Contains Only One Variable",
      "problem": "Evaluate the double integral ${∬}_{R}\\frac{1+y{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}A$,  where $R=[1,2]\\times [0,8].$",
      "worked_solution": "Using the linearity property of double integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}\\frac{1+y{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{R}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{R}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{R}y\\text{ }\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function under the first integral is independent of $y.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}A & =(8-0){\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}{|}_{1}^{2}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{8}-(-\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function under the second integral is independent of $x.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}y\\text{ }\\text{d}A & =(2-1){\\int }_{0}^{8}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}{|}_{0}^{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{{8}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, combining our results, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}\\frac{1+y{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}A=3+32=35.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices_complete": true,
          "choices": [
