  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14099811",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-81244",
      "knowledge_point_id": "307bccb6-036f-51bb-99ce-327aad8dfdbe",
      "knowledge_point": "Evaluating a Double Integral When the Integrand Contains Only One Variable",
      "problem": "Calculate ${∬}_{R}({x}^{2}-y+1)\\,\\text{d}A$,  where $R=[-3,0]\\times [0,4].$",
      "worked_solution": "Using the linearity property of double integrals, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}({x}^{2}-y+1)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{R}{x}^{2}\\text{ }\\text{d}A-{∬}_{R}y\\text{ }\\text{d}A+{∬}_{R}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function under the first integral is independent of $y.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}{x}^{2}\\text{ }\\text{d}A & =(4-0){\\int }_{-3}^{0}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]_{-3}^{0} \\\\  & =4(\\frac{{0}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{(-3)^{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function under the second integral is independent of $x.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}y\\text{ }\\text{d}A & =(0-(-3)){\\int }_{0}^{4}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =3[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =3(\\frac{{4}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third integral is the double integral of unity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}\\text{d}A=\\text{Area}(R)=3\\cdot 4=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, combining our results, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}({x}^{2}-y+1)\\,\\text{d}A=36-24+12=24.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices_complete": true,
