    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-130025",
      "knowledge_point_id": "8505beff-75b8-57bf-9312-e0fcf32f44fd",
      "knowledge_point": "Using Additivity of Double Integrals",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14099811/assets/ddc6c02aff017b31dd24ba6d47a3527aa46a2a5130cffc7094122545babd7656.png)\n\nConsider the region $R$ shown above. Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x, & (x,y)\\in S, \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in T, \\\\ {e}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in U.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A=\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that the domain $R$ can be represented as a union of three sub-domains, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & =S\\cup T\\cup U.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{S}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{T}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{U}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{T}0\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{U}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{U}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
