  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14099811",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-81253",
      "knowledge_point_id": "8059a197-e9fb-5306-8eed-62c0cea88f33",
      "knowledge_point": "Evaluating a Double Integral Using the Additivity Over a Rectangular Domain",
      "problem": "Evaluate the double integral ${∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A$, where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\, & \\text{if}\\,-1<x<1,\\,0<y<\\pi , \\\\ x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\, & \\text{if}\\,1<x<2,\\,0<y<\\pi ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand  $R$ is the domain of $f.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that we have $R={R}_{1}\\cup {R}_{2}$, where ${R}_{1}=(-1,1)\\times (0,\\pi )$ and ${R}_{2}=(1,2)\\times (0,\\pi ).$ So, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A= & ={∬}_{{R}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{{R}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{{R}_{1}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{{R}_{2}}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =(1-(-1)){\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y+({\\int }_{1}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x)({\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =2[\\operatorname{sin}⁡y]_{0}^{\\pi }+[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]_{1}^{2}\\cdot [-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y]_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =2(0)+(2-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (-(-1)+1) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices_complete": true,
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "\\frac{3}{2}",
              "option": "0",
              "source_option": "a"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "\\frac{5}{2}",
              "option": "1",
              "source_option": "b"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "2",
              "option": "2",
              "source_option": "c"
            },
