      "problem": "For the function $f(x,y,z)=z({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{-1/2},$ find the equation of the level surface $f(x,y,z)=1.$",
      "worked_solution": "The level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=1.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ z({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{-1/2} & =1 \\\\ z & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface for $c=1$ is the surface $z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$",
