  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14099948",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-36342",
      "knowledge_point_id": "5165085e-ffa9-533a-ab7c-c03304fc1489",
      "knowledge_point": "Describing a Level Surface of a Function",
      "problem": "Which of the following describes the level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)={e}^{-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}?$",
      "worked_solution": "The level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ {e}^{-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}} & =c \\\\ -{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡c \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $0<c\\le 1$ since $-\\operatorname{ln}⁡c$ must be non-negative. If we let $-\\operatorname{ln}⁡c={r}^{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surfaces form a family of spheres centered at the origin.",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices_complete": true,
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "A family of surfaces given by  ${z}^{2}+c={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ for $c>0$",
              "option": "0",
              "source_option": "a"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "A family of spheres centered at $(0,0,e)$",
              "option": "1",
              "source_option": "b"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "A family of spheres centered at $(0,0,{e}^{-1})$",
              "option": "2",
              "source_option": "c"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "A family of spheres centered at the origin",
              "option": "3",
              "source_option": "d"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "A family of surfaces given by ${z}^{2}+c={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ for $c\\ne 0$",
              "option": "4",
              "source_option": "e"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source_answer_policy": {
        "version": "revealed-ma-answer-v1",
        "feedback_sha256": "7813c4bea8a2bfe4e9257a9c0ef02232159308aab988668a9ecda900c33a3df8"
      },
      "difficulty": "moderate",
