      "knowledge_point_id": "2e891363-fea2-5de4-9bbb-67fddb077200",
      "knowledge_point": "Representing a Double Integral as a Repeated Integral When the Limits are Given",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14100085/assets/6dab892083ee6c9d0e77462cf2e5cf0f667e43b9c5ceb09aa4f6386d6e2f0c79.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $y={x}^{3}$ and $y=\\sqrt[3]{x},$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14100085/assets/8df9c4544478d7146dbcb1790c8ed9a9f57cd1e52b1f50a8b344c2aa0551fa90.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,{x}^{3}\\le y\\le \\sqrt[3]{x}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{3}}^{\\sqrt[3]{x}}f(x,y)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{{x}^{3}}^{\\sqrt[3]{x}}f(x,y)\\,dy]dx\\end{aligned}\n$$",
